#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af17da8-272b-55fb-8a5d-5857bd41051e" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "5537647b-d722-5000-8445-b2743cc1f1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1512" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n) =\\dfrac{6}{(-2)^n}$ for $n\\geq 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = r \\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{1}}=\\frac{6}{(-2)}=-3, \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(-3)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} f(n), \\qquad f(1) = -3, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac7a8723-02b7-5467-98f7-530ba8239c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "19d183f9-cf05-5477-8da9-642e2fd8e03b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1513" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = 1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1=1.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = 3.$\n\nSubstituting $a_1 = 1$ and $r = 3$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}\t\\[5pt]\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{n-1} \\[5pt]\n&= 3^{n-1}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133550 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f258f110-e541-5edf-8050-54d03177e499" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "6aa991c4-28e3-51fc-9642-19fa9346202d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4237" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $a_n = 8 \\cdot 3^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n.\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula,  we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =8\\cdot {3}^{1}=8\\cdot 3=24, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =8\\cdot {3}^{2}=8\\cdot 9=72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{72}{24}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric  sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3 a_n, \\quad a_1 = 24, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a910df9-7b4a-5892-a084-52d8b5dcd086" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "5027a265-fd62-574b-b6fa-2bacf9de7304" :question/math-academy-id "e-4238" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\nf(n+1) =-\\dfrac{f(n)}{3},\\, \\quad f(1) = {\\color{blue}9},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=-\\frac{f(n)}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n) =f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=9.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = {\\color{red}r} \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = -\\dfrac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1) = 9$ and $r = -\\dfrac{1}{3}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf(n) &= f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}\t\\[5pt]\n&= 9 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{n-1}.\n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =9\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{1})\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =-27\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-3)^{n}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{(-3)^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\dfrac{27}{(-3)^n} ,\\quad n\\geq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac7a8723-02b7-5467-98f7-530ba8239c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e7e8d4-1f2c-5731-9bb1-e3ca505e33e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551671} {:answer/id #uuid "3396d8a5-3041-5b5a-9e47-d23a76bcc657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551788} {:answer/id #uuid "4c334bbd-0f7b-5f52-a4a2-f06688b4334a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551846} {:answer/id #uuid "7582d12c-3842-52cf-9c33-8825aa815a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551942} {:answer/id #uuid "84778c37-5699-5591-895f-445665b6e0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03e7e8d4-1f2c-5731-9bb1-e3ca505e33e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551671} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e03764-6444-5a97-a79c-a332453344e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a2f8eb3-e0d7-5d85-8fb5-62ab15cba80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101752" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{5\\cdot 1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af17da8-272b-55fb-8a5d-5857bd41051e" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a3bc49-b244-599b-b0ff-1cbd8f81ac70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551687} {:answer/id #uuid "325d1297-2972-550e-b88d-db16dc9819fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551785} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc4548f-c1f5-5340-802b-4754d9b73c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551812} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e204ed-7f08-5596-a06e-74bc1aaea2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552192} {:answer/id #uuid "ec092f6f-dfdb-527f-a5bf-640a0c4dff59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=\\frac{3}{4}f(n),\\,f(1)=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3e204ed-7f08-5596-a06e-74bc1aaea2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552192} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff995c5-b742-5fa7-b249-c06283c96fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfd63bcb-672d-5a91-b2cf-d24e8a457ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-109868" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n)=\\frac{6}{(-2)^{n+1}}$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $f(1).$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{1+1}}=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{2+1}}=\\frac{6}{(-8)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-\\frac{6}{12}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a910df9-7b4a-5892-a084-52d8b5dcd086" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c48d543-2d3c-51bb-a7bf-0270b3661c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{2}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551847} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8329a8-9240-5aad-b604-d9ff98436aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)={24}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552041} {:answer/id #uuid "cc85700b-91d3-5f37-a0fd-d0b78d1f06ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{2}{{24}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552154} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c2c41c-0f75-5fa4-a688-22bb4ee3f293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{4}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552199} {:answer/id #uuid "e877ea80-a0f4-50cc-b19e-fc100855d57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e877ea80-a0f4-50cc-b19e-fc100855d57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552207} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d6b8a21-70e2-5b57-a0a2-6cb7c4f3a1b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a040fd0d-33b9-5cd2-ba84-4a64f3c5606d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109880" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6},\\,\\,f(1)=4,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=4.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=4$ and $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{{6}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f258f110-e541-5edf-8050-54d03177e499" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5df3b3-29db-5dff-9c0f-a4979ae11c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5df3b3-29db-5dff-9c0f-a4979ae11c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551769} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cd3f92-3539-51de-aaee-ec6c6469d869" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552275} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fd67fa-66fe-5f9b-b331-094ee4e75fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4a_n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14fd67fa-66fe-5f9b-b331-094ee4e75fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4a_n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551708} :answer-field/id #uuid "54150f3b-cbd7-542d-9833-4701c3e58b79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1246524a-95f9-5d04-bcaf-769f77cf931a" :question/math-academy-id "q-251874" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is ${a}_{n}=5\\cdot {4}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the recursive formula for this sequence? Express your answer in terms of ${a}_{n}.$\n\n${a}_{n+1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{a}_{1}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term ${a}_{1}.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =5\\cdot {4}^{1}=5\\cdot 4=20, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =5\\cdot {4}^{2}=5\\cdot 16=80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{80}{20}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af17da8-272b-55fb-8a5d-5857bd41051e" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78aa06cf-4bd4-5fbb-bcef-d03d0bd82a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78aa06cf-4bd4-5fbb-bcef-d03d0bd82a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551950} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff13047-2dda-5ef9-be73-5aa85393fd61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9b06f56-a0fd-5569-9b2b-f6bf5464e530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5f(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b06f56-a0fd-5569-9b2b-f6bf5464e530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5f(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552247} :answer-field/id #uuid "4103e96a-092e-552a-9617-6fa0074bd918" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9893d5c-f0eb-5a20-a7b0-6d7123b5cbef" :question/math-academy-id "q-252169" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n)=10\\cdot (-5)^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the recursive formula for this sequence? Express your answer in terms of $f(n).$\n\n$f(n+1)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,f(1)=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $f(1).$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =10\\cdot (-5)^{1}=10\\cdot (-5)=-50, \\\\ f(2) & =10\\cdot (-5)^{2}=10\\cdot 25=250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{250}{(-50)}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=-5f(n),\\,f(1)=-50,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac7a8723-02b7-5467-98f7-530ba8239c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4060772b-3de8-5534-b56c-8b7a37476bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{7}{{3}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551821} {:answer/id #uuid "53b52c4e-fecf-5906-b0d1-f38305a89134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {7}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551863} {:answer/id #uuid "63d5d4e3-2de8-53a3-8ffc-a054da079d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {3}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551901} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bb07b2-92a7-55ae-ba89-d2cfd3c82595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552099} {:answer/id #uuid "c94b2131-8119-57cb-89b4-783c4f703e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94b2131-8119-57cb-89b4-783c4f703e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552145} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b6e58-02ae-5c02-8cb3-0f391e390f10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98925317-9d30-5e69-bd45-e05c5755cffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-44987" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{7},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=3.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=3$ and $r=\\frac{1}{7}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{7}^{n}}) \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{1}{{7}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f258f110-e541-5edf-8050-54d03177e499" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160414de-5290-5c26-b029-5ab91f757639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=0.1{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=0.2,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551712} {:answer/id #uuid "792d1485-53c1-50cd-b8bf-20cc8f30610a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=0.1,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551951} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6bbf28-a239-525f-a22d-bd386b86ae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551967} {:answer/id #uuid "82324573-3d9f-5ab5-a596-73b5f929059b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=0.2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551973} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c5561-921c-5de3-a42d-37e3645028e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=0.2,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75c5561-921c-5de3-a42d-37e3645028e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=0.2,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552108} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b71161-13e8-5b78-82dd-328751c6dd6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e36afb6e-8973-5d11-97ae-d34a93970838" :question/math-academy-id "q-44989" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of an geometric sequence is ${a}_{n}=0.1\\cdot {2}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for an geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term ${a}_{1}.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =0.1\\cdot {2}^{1}=0.2, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =0.1\\cdot {2}^{2}=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric  sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & =2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=0.2,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a910df9-7b4a-5892-a084-52d8b5dcd086" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e87a69a-83da-50aa-90f7-3e2c57996375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{87}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551730} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8a985f-6cf4-5d99-a382-0c90a98f52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{6}{{78}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551848} {:answer/id #uuid "6db750dc-fe88-55f5-8fc6-6406faf86ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{78}{{13}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551924} {:answer/id #uuid "9bee3c06-979d-5760-b511-9139eafe8ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{6}{{13}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552043} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0cfecd-0318-5e34-9d02-cc04ef5ef74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0cfecd-0318-5e34-9d02-cc04ef5ef74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552148} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7fe9ae-7173-5401-b140-6234f32b66d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e4e0f75-9894-567b-a1a8-c012ad934066" :question/math-academy-id "q-73268" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6},\\,\\,f(1)=13,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=13.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=13$ and $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =78\\cdot \\frac{1}{{6}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}]}
